# Which tests a changed path needs in this repository, for the shell profile.
# Read by `jig verify` when it narrows (ADR-0041; schemas/verify-map.md).
#
#   <glob>  <decision>
#
# The first line whose glob matches a path decides it. A decision is `-`
# (the path affects no test), `ALL` (run everything), or tests/run.sh
# filters. `**` and `*` both match across `/`. A path no line matches is
# decided by the profile's built-in rules: a script maps to the test file
# named after it, anything else runs everything.
#
# A `-` is the one decision a run cannot confirm: it claims the path reaches
# no test, and a targeted run is green whether that is true or the filter was
# aimed elsewhere. Argue one the other way round, by damaging something the
# path must reach and watching the red land where the line says
# (conventions/detectors.md).

# The session hook has no test file of its own; its tests live with the
# command it triggers.
scripts/jig-session-hook        housekeeping::
# The dispatcher: argument routing, --help, version. Commands are tested in
# their own files.
scripts/jig                     dispatcher::
scripts/jig.cmd                 init:: install::

adapters/**                     adapters::
profiles/**                     profiles:: verify::
# One template is read by a third suite: tests/adapters.t.sh opens
# templates/AGENTS.md directly, both for the marked region's own
# well-formedness and to hold docs/install.mdx's pasted copy against it.
# Measured: damaging that region turns adapters:: red, and the line below
# does not name it.
templates/AGENTS.md             init:: upgrade:: adapters::
templates/**                    init:: upgrade::

# A skill description is what a runtime routes by, and the routing evals
# score it: a SKILL.md reads as documentation, which would reach no test.
skills/*/SKILL.md               routing::
tests/routing/**                routing::

# A library is tested through every script that sources it, not only the
# first one that did: release-lib.sh decides "already released" for the
# release tag, the epic gate and the changelog gate alike.
.github/scripts/release-lib.sh  release-tag:: epic-pr-check:: changelog-check::
# CI configuration is exercised by CI itself, not by tests/run.sh — except the
# one thing a run cannot see about its own file: a Windows suite whose shares
# do not cover it runs what it was told and reports green on the part it ran.
.github/workflows/**            ci-workflow::

install.ps1                     install::
tests/*.t.ps1                   install::

# Tests run in their own temporary repositories with their own files; this
# repository's settings never reach them.
.gitignore                      -
.gitattributes                  -
.claude/settings.json           -
.ai/config.yaml                 -
# The same, for the record of what is installed here. This repository
# installs itself in link mode, so `jig upgrade` rewrites .ai/manifest
# whenever a framework-owned file is placed, which is often; unnamed, it
# fell to the built-in rule for an unknown path and ran the whole suite.
# That is the run this map's gap was found in — four changed files, one of
# them this one, 2065 tests — where the other three together ask for 111.
# What reads this file is `jig verify`'s own staleness gate, not a test: it
# refuses before any profile runs when the manifest says a framework file
# is still to be placed. Measured: with this file overwritten with one line
# of junk, init::, upgrade::, status::, doctor::, adapters::, section::,
# ci-windows-scope:: and knowledge:: stayed green, 610 tests.
.ai/manifest                    -

# Except this map: tests/verify.t.sh checks it, and CI reads it to decide
# how much of the suite a change needs (.github/scripts/ci-scope.sh).
.ai/verify/**                   verify::test_verify_own_shell_map

# --- prose ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shell profile already answers "no test" for *.md and docs/*, so the
# four lines below change no verdict on their own. They are here because a
# `-` is a claim about this repository that someone must be able to read,
# review and disprove; inherited from a built-in, the same claim is made
# silently, by a file this project does not own.

# This repository's own instructions. Every test that touches an AGENTS.md
# either plants one in its temporary repository or reads the one `jig init`
# generated there from templates/AGENTS.md; none reads this file. The marked
# section it takes part in is tested through templates/AGENTS.md and
# scripts/lib/section.sh, which have their own lines above — naming those
# tests here would run 154 of them for a change none of them can see.
# What would end this: fifteen tests build a throwaway install source with
# `cp -R "$JIG_HOME"/.`, so the day `init` or `upgrade` reads
# <source>/AGENTS.md, this file reaches them without a word.
AGENTS.md                       -
CLAUDE.md                       -
CONTRIBUTING.md                 -
README.md                       -

# The documentation site, with one page that is not prose. The Jig section of
# docs/install.mdx is a copy of the marked region of templates/AGENTS.md, for
# a reader who has to paste it by hand, and one test is what keeps the copy
# from drifting. Left to the built-in rule for docs/*, that test ran on no
# change to the page it guards.
docs/install.mdx                adapters::test_docs_agents_section_matches_the_template
docs/**                         -
